From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 09:45:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] nir/opt_vectorize_io: merge xfb elements a dword apart, in
 order

vectorize_store() merged the xfb elements of the scalar stores it replaces by
testing out[i].offset == out[j].offset + xfb_comp_size * (j - i), with
xfb_comp_size the store's bit size (or 32 for mediump, which upconverts).
That is wrong twice: nir_io_xfb::offset counts dwords, not bits, so the stride
is 16 or 32 where consecutive components of a run are one dword apart, and the
comparison is reversed, requiring the later component j at the lower offset.
An ascending run - every real one - therefore never merged, so the pass has
never vectorized xfb at all, while a descending pair exactly one bit size
apart did merge, and merged wrongly.

The two halves come together as soon as nir_opt_varyings packs captured
scalars into one location. An interleaved capture list already gives a
location's components non-monotonic offsets once it contains an interior
gl_SkipComponents1, and compaction then packs components from different
locations together with no ordering at all. In
KHR-GL46.enhanced_layouts.xfb_struct_explicit_location_geometry the packed
location ends up capturing dwords 32, 0, 33 and 2, and 32 == 0 + 32 * 1 hits
the predicate exactly. Component 1 is folded into component 0's run and
zeroed, so the scalar that belongs at dword 0 is no longer written there: the
now two-component run at dword 32 emits it at dword 33 instead, on top of the
capture that belongs at 33. The next nir_opt_varyings_bulk rescalarizes the
run and the shifted offset becomes the store's own, so the record permanently
loses its first scalar and duplicates its third.

Merge on the only relation a nir_io_xfb can express instead. One struct
describes four components of one location going to one buffer, out[] indexed
by start component and offset counted in dwords, and the low and high 16 bits
of a location live in separate structs (xfb[0] and xfb[1]), so components that
are contiguous in the buffer are always exactly one dword apart inside a
single struct. A run starting at i continues at j only where j is captured, to
the same buffer, at offset[i] + (j - i); no bit size can enter that relation,
which is why the mediump case disappears rather than being corrected.
Uncaptured components and components captured at unrelated offsets each keep
their own element, which is what the pass emits for them today, so the only
behaviour that changes is that genuine runs now merge and impossible ones no
longer do.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_vectorize_io.c | 28 +++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_vectorize_io.c b/src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_vectorize_io.c
index 0669b6e9a11cc55ab2d84a082e455aa7a999123d..bcebafc13236688e7e626510b741d0153124b592 100644
--- a/src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_vectorize_io.c
+++ b/src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_vectorize_io.c
@@ -260,18 +260,28 @@ vectorize_store(nir_intrinsic_instr *chan[8], unsigned start, unsigned count,
                 step != merge_low_high_16_to_32);
       }
 
-      /* Now vectorize xfb info by merging the individual elements. */
+      /* Now vectorize xfb info by merging the individual elements.
+       *
+       * nir_io_xfb::offset counts dwords, and one nir_io_xfb describes one
+       * set of four components that are one dword apart each (the low and
+       * the high 16 bits of a location live in xfb[0] and xfb[1], so a run
+       * never straddles them). A run therefore continues at component j only
+       * where j is also captured, into the same buffer, at exactly j - i
+       * dwords past the component the run starts at. Components captured at
+       * unrelated offsets - which an interleaved capture list produces as
+       * soon as it contains a gl_SkipComponents hole, and which a location
+       * packed by the varying compactor produces with no ordering at all -
+       * must each keep their own element.
+       */
       for (unsigned i = start; i < start + count; i++) {
-         /* mediump means that xfb upconverts to 32 bits when writing to
-          * memory.
-          */
-         unsigned xfb_comp_size =
-            nir_intrinsic_io_semantics(chan[i]).medium_precision ? 32 : chan[i]->src[0].ssa->bit_size;
+         if (!xfb[i / 4].out[i % 4].num_components)
+            continue;
 
          for (unsigned j = i + 1; j < start + count; j++) {
-            if (xfb[i / 4].out[i % 4].buffer != xfb[j / 4].out[j % 4].buffer ||
-                xfb[i / 4].out[i % 4].offset != xfb[j / 4].out[j % 4].offset +
-                                                   xfb_comp_size * (j - i))
+            if (!xfb[j / 4].out[j % 4].num_components ||
+                xfb[i / 4].out[i % 4].buffer != xfb[j / 4].out[j % 4].buffer ||
+                xfb[j / 4].out[j % 4].offset !=
+                   xfb[i / 4].out[i % 4].offset + (j - i))
                break;
 
             xfb[i / 4].out[i % 4].num_components++;
